# Architecture

Current Seed S0–S1 uses the finite symbolic engine and existing local Lab/journal.
The neural architecture below describes the preserved research track; it does
not require adding PPO, a neural parser or a new memory stack to Seed. Distinguish
supplied primitives, taught evidence, finite inference and measured capabilities.

## Level 1 — Task intelligence

Actor–critic RL: PPO first; A2C, SAC, and DQN where appropriate. Include neural state representations, memory, planning, and continual learning. The actor chooses actions; the critic evaluates states; learning comes from environment feedback.

## Level 2 — Meta-intelligence

Proposes architectures (depth, width, residual/attention), activations, learning rates, optimizers, memory, training configs, and alternative learning strategies. Selects experiments from prior evidence, compute cost, and predicted research value. Start with contextual bandits; use RL when enough experiment data exists.

## Neural Architecture Factory

Build PyTorch models from structured specs. Investigate NAS, evolution (mutation/crossover), PBT, modular/learnable components, efficient search, and transfer of successful architectures. **Judge trained models**, not untrained proposals.

## Activation discovery

Compose primitives (add/mul, protected div, sigmoid, tanh, softplus, sin/cos, bounded exp, learnable scalars). Example: `f(x) = x * sigmoid(a*x) + b * tanh(x)`. Every candidate needs stability tests, gradient checks, autograd verification, training, independent eval, and comparison to ReLU/GELU/SiLU. Optimize for experimental usefulness, not novelty of formulas.

LLMs may help planning and code. They must not replace neural training, evolution, or independent evaluation.
